%S A399977 1,1,2,2,3,4,6,4,4,7,9,4,3,6,6,3,1,1,2,2,4,4,6,4,2,4,4,2,5,7,5,1,1,1,
%T A399977 2,2,4,4,6,4,3,6,4,2,6,7,6,2,1,1,2,2,3,4,7,4,5,7,7,4,7,9,6,2,4,2,2,2,
%U A399977 4,4,6,4,3,8,5,6,9,9,6,2,3,1,2,2,3,4,7
%N A399977 a(n) gives the number of appropriate values when computing A399633(n).
%C A399977 Of course, we will always take the smallest appropriate value to extend A399633.
%H A399977 Rémy Sigrist, <a href="/A399977/b399977.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A399977 Rémy Sigrist, <a href="/A399977/a399977.txt">C++ program</a>
%F A399977 a(n) > 0.
%e A399977 For n = 5: the first 4 terms of A399633 are 0, 1, 1, 2.
%e A399977 We could extend A399633 with the values 1, 3 and 4:
%e A399977                                          4
%e A399977                            3             |
%e A399977            2             2 |           2 |
%e A399977        1 1 |         1 1 | |       1 1 | |
%e A399977      0 | | | 1     0 | | | |     0 | | | |
%e A399977 Any other value would not be appropriate.
%e A399977 So a(5) = 3.
%o A399977 (C++) // See Links section.
%Y A399977 Cf. A399633.
%K A399977 nonn,new
%O A399977 1,3
